{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>And HaShem spoke, etc.</b> One needs to know what HaShem spoke to them. Further, one needs to know the intent of His saying <b>and He charged them unto</b>, etc. And our Sages of blessed memory (Exodus Rabbah 7:3) interpreted that Israel should conduct themselves gently and also conduct themselves with honor toward the kingdom, and this is the way of derash. Further, it is difficult: where is the response of El Elyon to the words of His emissary, all the more so since he advanced an argument that is audibly compelling — and even if one should say he erred, and the a fortiori argument is not truly an a fortiori argument, He should still have refuted his words. And although we might say that the absence of a response is itself the refutation of the a fortiori argument, we do not accept this. With all this, in what lies before us we cannot say so, by noting one observation in the verse — that after this comes ״וַיְדַבֵּר ה׳״ etc.: if we say that what Moses said, ״הֵן בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״, El Elyon did not bother to answer, if so, why did HaShem not wish to speak the things stated in the verse ״וַיְדַבֵּר״ etc., and also this charge that He stated, ״וַיְצַוֵּם״, until after Moses had spoken before Him ״הֵן בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״?" "<b>Truly,</b> the correct view in my eyes is that He came to answer him regarding his complaint that Israel did not regard him enough to listen to his words, and further regarding the side of Pharaoh — that when Moses speaks, he has a deficiency and shortcoming on account of being עֲרַל שְׂפָתַיִם (uncircumcised of lips), as I have explained. And the response to this came: <b>And HaShem spoke unto Moses and unto Aaron</b> — meaning, He made them equal in the mission, and by this Aaron would also speak to Pharaoh, for the two of them are equal in the matter. And regarding the fact that they would not be regarded enough to be listened to, He said <b>and He charged them</b> — meaning, He enthroned them over the children of Israel and over Pharaoh, after the manner of (1 Samuel 13:14) ״וַיְצַוֵּהוּ ה׳ לְנָגִיד״, and from now on, behold, their awe is upon Israel and upon Pharaoh." "And He said <b>to bring out</b>, etc. — meaning, for the sake of this particular matter HaShem endowed them with the quality of kingship over Israel, so that they would not refuse to leave, and over Pharaoh, so that he would not hold them back through them. From here you learn that Moses and Aaron have authority to exercise dominion over any Israelite who refuses to leave. And perhaps Moses intended precisely this when he said ״בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל לֹא שָׁמְעוּ אֵלַי״ — meaning, they did not wish to leave, for they said ״יָנִיחַ לָנוּ בֶּן עַמְרָם בִּמְקוֹמֵנוּ״. For this reason HaShem enthroned the brothers as kings over them, as mentioned, and from now on the argument of ״הֵן בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״ etc. took flight, for from now onward the awe of kingship is upon them and upon Pharaoh, and Moses will speak to him as one speaks to his servant — and so it was. And with this the matters are properly resolved."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:25:19.991001076Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}